// Darwin OS-thread provider for the Stage-2 port of runtime/sched/'s worker
// pool (#1403). See ../thread.bit for the shared contract, the seam against
// #1364, and the three rules a child thread's body must obey.
//
// `pthread_create` through libSystem rather than a raw syscall (§11.7), for the
// reason the whole Darwin side of the runtime uses externs: Apple publishes no
// stable syscall numbers and reserves the right to renumber them, so there is
// nothing to encode against. `syscall` is rejected outright when targeting
// Darwin, so there is no other path even in principle.
//
// THIS PROVIDER IS MUCH SHORTER THAN THE LINUX ONE, and the asymmetry is real
// rather than an omission. `pthread_create` allocates and guards the child's
// stack itself and enters the body through an ordinary C call, so the two
// things that dominate the Linux file — mapping a guarded stack, and the `asm`
// block that lets a raw `clone` child branch to an entry it can never return
// from — have no counterpart here. What both files DO share is the contract,
// and that is what ../thread.bit specifies.
//
// This module's only import is `runningSentinel` from ../thread.bit, pulled up
// from a per-provider duplicate by #2553 — see provider-duplication.md's
// `thread` section.
import { runningSentinel } from "../../thread"

// `pthread_create(pthread_t *thread, const pthread_attr_t *attr,
//                 void *(*start)(void *), void *arg) -> int`
//
// `attr` is passed as nil, which selects the platform defaults — including a
// guarded stack, which is why this provider maps nothing of its own. `start` is
// a C function pointer, which is exactly what `entryOf` (§11.10) yields.
//
// `pthread_t` is an opaque pointer, taken here as `*i64` — the out-parameter is
// one pointer-width word and a `[]i64(1)` element IS eight contiguous bytes,
// unlike a `[]u8`, which a Bit slice stores one byte per 8-byte word (§11.8).
// That distinction has already produced two silent bugs in this runtime port.
extern fn pthread_create(th: *i64, attr: *u8, start: *byte, arg: *byte): i32

// `pthread_join(pthread_t thread, void **value_ptr) -> int`
//
// Reaps a finished thread. NOT used as the join primitive — see `threadRelease`
// for why it is the reaper rather than the waiter.
extern fn pthread_join(th: i64, value: *u8): i32

// ---- the child's exit path (#1846) ---------------------------------------
//
// `bit_rt_gc_thread_exit` releases this OS thread's mutator slot (ABI.md §6). A
// child that ALLOCATES claims one lazily (#1431), and a leaked slot stays
// `running` forever — which corrupts nothing but makes every later rendezvous
// expire, i.e. collection quietly stops. Measured here before the fix:
// `_tests_/stress/gcslotexitdarwin` completed 1 collection and ABANDONED 625,
// against 510 completed and 0 abandoned on the previous runtime.
//
// THE OBLIGATION BELONGS HERE, NOT IN THE CHILD BODY, for the reason
// ../linux/spawn.bit states at length: asking "user code that starts a raw OS
// thread" to make the call is a rule every future caller must remember whose
// penalty for forgetting is silent. This provider owns the child's entry, so it
// is the one place that can discharge it unconditionally.
//
// WHY A TRAMPOLINE AND NOT `runtime/root/`'S pthread_key DESTRUCTOR. `runtime/root/` arms
// a TSD destructor AT SLOT CLAIM, which is strictly stronger — it covers threads
// this runtime never started. That is not expressible here: the claim path
// (`../../gc`'s `currentMutatorOn`) is `@nosplit`, and `@nosplit` rejects both an
// `extern` call and a `syscall` (E0075), so `pthread_setspecific` cannot be
// reached from it. Arming from the PARENT in `threadStart` is not a substitute
// either, since TSD is per-thread and would arm the parent's slot.
//
// THE GAP THAT LEAVES, stated rather than discovered later: a thread this
// provider did not create still leaks its slot. Nothing in the tree creates one —
// `threadStart`'s only caller in `runtime/` and `stdlib/` is boot's worker
// bring-up, and `workerBody` brackets itself with `gcThreadEnter`/`gcThreadExit`
// already — and a Bit binary embeds no foreign thread source. If one ever
// appears, this is the comment that says what to fix.
//
// Declared and called directly rather than imported from `../../gc`: the live
// registry in a normally linked program is `runtime/root/`'s, not the Bit module's,
// whose `worldAddr` is bound only by `runtime/root`'s own boot. Same call and
// same reason as ../linux/spawn.bit's.
extern fn bit_rt_gc_thread_exit()

// `int` -> `*i64` and `int` -> `*byte`. A leaf may not import ../../sched's
// `taskAt`, so the two casts are spelled here the same way it spells its one.
@nosplit fn wordsAt(addr: int): *i64 {
  let z: *i64 = nil
  return z + addr / 8
}

@nosplit fn bytesAt(addr: int): *byte {
  let z: *byte = nil
  return z + addr
}

// `int` -> `*i32`, for reconstructing `done`'s address inside the child. A
// `*i32` rather than `*i64` because `done` itself is `*i32` (../thread.bit's
// `threadRunning` is a 32-bit word so `atomicLoad`'s poll in `threadJoin` can
// use a 4-byte op). Same shape as ../linux/spawn.bit's `i32Ptr`.
@nosplit fn i32PtrAt(addr: int): *i32 {
  let z: *i32 = nil
  return z + addr / 4
}

// Call the raw code address `entry` with `arg` in the first-argument register
// (x0 / rdi) and return normally. A copy of ../../sched's `taskInvoke` for the
// same reason and with the same encoding — `entry` is a bare code pointer, not a
// `(code, env)` function value, so no `call_value` reaches it. `blr`/`call`
// rather than a tail branch because the callee returns here and this thread's
// exit path must run after it.
//
// The clobber list names every caller-saved GPR the call destroys, not just
// x30 (#3916) — `threadTrampoline`'s `done` is a Bit local live across this
// call, and an under-declared list let the allocator park it in a
// caller-saved register the callee is free to overwrite. That was live and
// silent: only an unrelated neighbouring `bl` next to this `blr` happened to
// force callee-saved allocation instead (#3878). `entry`/`arg` occupy x9/x0
// (rax/rdi) and are excluded as inputs, not re-listed as clobbers.
@nosplit fn invokeRaw(entry: int, arg: int) {
  asm {
    arm64 { 0xD63F0120 }        // blr x9
    x64   { 0xFF, 0xD0 }        // call rax
    input arm64 x9 x64 rax = entry
    input arm64 x0 x64 rdi = arg
    clobber arm64 { x1, x2, x3, x4, x5, x6, x7, x8, x10, x11, x12, x13, x14, x15, x16, x17, x18, x30, memory }
    clobber x64 { rcx, rdx, rsi, r8, r9, r10, r11, memory }
  }
}

// `pthread_create` carries ONE `void *` to the child, and the trampoline needs
// three words — the real entry, the real argument, and `done`'s address, which
// the child must clear itself (see `threadStart`'s comment and ../thread.bit's
// `threadRunning` — pthreads has no equivalent of Linux's `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID`,
// so nothing else can). They come from a fixed pool rather than an allocator: a
// provider is a leaf with no allocator to call, and a static pool keeps this off
// the "no unbounded allocation after startup" list.
//
// Three words per cell, and WORD 0 IS THE CLAIM FLAG — a cell is free exactly
// when its entry word is 0, which no real `entryOf` ever is. That removes the
// fourth word an explicit in-use flag would need and makes claiming a single CAS.
const trampMax: int = 64
let trampCells: [192]i64

// Reserve a cell for `(start, arg, done)`, returning its address or 0 when the
// pool is full. Bounded by `trampMax`.
//
// `@nosplit` (#3761): every caller reaches this while holding `scRegLock`
// (`../../sched/worker.bit`'s `schedStartWorker`, and `../../root/darwin/`'s
// post-boot growth path via `schedGrowArm`/`schedGrowCommit`) — the same word
// `stwScanTasks` takes to enumerate roots on every collection. A plain `fn`
// gets a safepoint poll on this loop's back edge, so a thread that ever took
// that edge (the pool not immediately free) could park, or become the
// collector, while holding a lock the collector's own root scan needs —
// deadlock either way. `@nosplit` removes only that back-edge poll (§10.3.1);
// the loop itself is still bounded by `trampMax`. The body qualifies without
// change: `ptrOf`, `atomicCmpxchg`, pointer arithmetic, and `int(p)` are all
// on the `@nosplit` allowlist, and the E0075 check below is what proves it
// rather than this comment.
@nosplit fn trampClaim(start: int, arg: int, done: int): int {
  let p: *i64 = ptrOf(trampCells)
  let i = 0
  while (i < trampMax) {
    if (atomicCmpxchg(p + i * 3, 0, start)) {
      // Safe unsynchronised: the CAS above makes this cell ours until the child
      // releases it, and the child cannot run before `pthread_create`.
      *(p + i * 3 + 1) = arg
      *(p + i * 3 + 2) = done
      return int(p) + i * 24
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return 0
}

// Release a cell claimed for `start`. The CAS form, not a plain store, so a
// release can never zero a cell some other thread has since claimed.
fn trampRelease(cell: int, start: int) {
  let p = wordsAt(cell)
  atomicCmpxchg(p, start, 0)
}

// Every OS thread this provider starts enters here, not at the caller's body.
//
// NOT `@nosplit`: that attribute forbids the `extern` call this exists to make.
// The cell is released BEFORE the body runs, so a long-lived thread does not
// hold a pool entry for its whole life and the pool bounds concurrent STARTS
// rather than concurrent threads.
fn threadTrampoline(cell: *byte): *byte {
  let addr = int(cell)
  let p = wordsAt(addr)
  let start = *p
  let arg = *(p + 1)
  // `done`'s address is read into a local BEFORE the release below, because
  // releasing the cell makes it eligible for a concurrent `trampClaim` to
  // overwrite before this function reads it again.
  let done = *(p + 2)
  trampRelease(addr, start)
  invokeRaw(start, arg)
  // Release this thread's mutator slot first, then clear `done` as the very
  // last Bit code this thread runs — same order as ../linux/spawn.bit's
  // epilogue-then-ctid-clear, so a joiner observing `done == 0` also observes
  // (modulo the residual gap `threadRelease`'s comment names) a thread that
  // has finished tearing itself down, not merely finished the caller's body.
  bit_rt_gc_thread_exit()
  atomicStore(i32PtrAt(done), i32(0))
  let z: *byte = nil
  return z
}

// Start an OS thread running `start(arg)`. Returns an opaque handle (> 0) for
// `threadRelease`, or 0 on failure.
//
// `start` must be `entryOf(f)` for a named `function f(arg: *byte): *byte`
// obeying the child rules in ../thread.bit. `done` is a caller-owned word this
// function arms; THE CHILD ITSELF must clear it before returning, because
// pthreads has no equivalent of Linux's `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` and nothing else
// can. That obligation is the one asymmetry in the contract and it is stated on
// `threadRunning` in ../thread.bit.
//
// The handle is the `pthread_t`. That is what actually has to be released on
// this platform — see ../thread.bit on why the handle is opaque and why a
// caller must not read meaning into the number.
export @symbol("bit_rt_port_thread_start") fn threadStart(start: *byte, arg: *byte, done: *i32): int {
  let slot = []i64(1)
  slot[0] = 0

  // Armed BEFORE the create, not after: the child can run to completion and
  // clear this word before `pthread_create` has even returned. Arming it
  // afterwards would overwrite the child's 0 with 1 and strand every joiner
  // until its deadline.
  atomicStore(done, i32(runningSentinel()))

  // The child enters `threadTrampoline`, which calls `start(arg)`, releases
  // this thread's mutator slot (#1846), and clears `done` (#3375). A cell
  // carries the triple through pthread's single `void *`.
  let cell = trampClaim(int(start), int(arg), int(done))
  if (cell == 0) {
    // Pool exhausted: `trampMax` starts are already in flight. Refused rather
    // than started unwrapped, because an unwrapped child leaks its slot and the
    // penalty for that is silent.
    atomicStore(done, i32(0))
    return 0
  }

  let noattr: *u8 = nil
  let rc = int(pthread_create(ptrOf(slot), noattr, entryOf(threadTrampoline), bytesAt(cell)))
  if (rc != 0) {
    // No thread exists, so nothing will ever clear `done`. Disarm it so a
    // caller that joins anyway gets `threadJoinDone` immediately instead of
    // waiting out a full deadline for a thread that was never created. The cell
    // must go back too — no child will ever release it.
    trampRelease(cell, int(start))
    atomicStore(done, i32(0))
    return 0
  }

  let handle = int(slot[0])
  if (handle == 0) {
    // pthread_create reported success, so a thread is running and its `done`
    // word will be cleared; only the handle is unusable. Reporting 0 here would
    // claim no thread exists, so this is treated as the impossible case it is
    // rather than silently mapped onto failure.
    return 0
  }
  return handle
}

// Release a finished thread's resources. Call only after `threadJoin` has
// reported `threadJoinDone`.
//
// WHY `pthread_join` IS THE REAPER AND NOT THE WAITER. `pthread_join` has no
// timed form — POSIX offers no `pthread_timedjoin` and Darwin adds none — so
// using it to WAIT would make the wait unbounded, and a worker that failed to
// exit would hang the caller instead of reporting it. ../thread.bit's join is a
// bounded poll of the `done` word for exactly that reason. By the time this is
// called the child has already published its completion, so the join here
// returns essentially immediately and serves only to reclaim the thread's
// resources, which a never-joined, never-detached pthread would leak.
//
// The residual gap is small and worth naming: the child clears `done` a few
// instructions before libSystem's teardown finishes, so this may briefly wait
// out that tail. It cannot wait indefinitely — the child has, by construction,
// already reached the end of its body.
export @symbol("bit_rt_port_thread_release") fn threadRelease(handle: int): bool {
  if (handle == 0) {
    return false
  }
  let novalue: *u8 = nil
  return int(pthread_join(i64(handle), novalue)) == 0
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Publishing a thread's real stack top (#2648)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `pthread_self() -> pthread_t` and `pthread_get_stackaddr_np(pthread_t) ->
// void *`. `pthread_t` is the same opaque handle `pthread_join` above already
// takes as `i64`. `pthread_get_stackaddr_np` answers the HIGHEST address of
// the named thread's stack — Darwin stacks grow down, so this is the top, not
// the base — and it answers for ANY thread libSystem knows about: the one it
// sets up for `main` before this runtime's own entry ever runs, and every
// pthread `threadStart` above creates. That is the whole reason this door is
// so much shorter than ../linux/spawn.bit's: the OS already tracks the
// mapping, so there is no bounded table to claim and release here.
extern fn pthread_self(): i64
extern fn pthread_get_stackaddr_np(thread: i64): *u8
extern fn pthread_get_stacksize_np(thread: i64): u64

// This thread's real stack top, or 0 when the platform call reports none.
// Never a fixed window above `sp` — see ../linux/spawn.bit's header for why
// that shape is unsafe: past the real top is unmapped, and a faulting
// collector is worse than the sweep it prevents. `int(...)` on a null `void *`
// is already 0, so no separate check is needed to honor "return 0 when
// unknown".
//
// Pinned like every other cross-module door in this file
// (`bit_rt_port_thread_start`/`_release`): its one intended caller,
// `../../gc/gcworldsync.bit`'s claim path (#2649), is a different module, and
// an unpinned export there would be emitted as `m<N>$threadStackTop`, a name
// no separately emitted object can define (SPEC §11.9). The signature and the
// meaning match ../linux/spawn.bit's door of the same name exactly, because
// one caller imports whichever platform module is built.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_thread_stack_top") fn threadStackTop(): int {
  return int(pthread_get_stackaddr_np(pthread_self()))
}

// This thread's real stack BASE — the low end of the same mapping
// `threadStackTop` names the high end of (#3516). `pthread_get_stacksize_np`
// answers the SAME thread's reservation size in bytes; Darwin stacks grow
// down, so subtracting it from the top lands on the base. 0 when the top
// itself is unknown (a null `pthread_get_stackaddr_np` already makes the
// subtraction land on a negative/bogus value, so that case is checked
// explicitly rather than trusted to fall out of the arithmetic).
//
// Pinned for the same reason `threadStackTop` above is: its one intended
// caller is a different module (`../../gc/gcworldsync.bit`'s claim path).
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_thread_stack_base") fn threadStackBase(): int {
  let top = int(pthread_get_stackaddr_np(pthread_self()))
  if (top == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  return top - int(pthread_get_stacksize_np(pthread_self()))
}
